WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 63: The Wright Brothers at Huffman Prairie: Mastering Practical Flight

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.760
[Rebecca] Welcome back to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:07.040
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:21.200
[Rebecca] Every American schoolchild learns the story of December 17, 193, when Orville and Wilbur Wright made history's first powered, controlled, heavier-than-air airplane flights over the windswept sand dunes of Kitty Hawk, North Carolina.

4
00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:27.440
[Rebecca] But those four brief hops, lasting barely a minute combined, did not create a practical airplane.

5
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:39.360
[Rebecca] The true, grueling and revolutionary work of mastering aviation took place not on an ocean beach, but in a bumpy, swampy cow pasture eight miles east of Dayton, Ohio, called Huffman Prairie.

6
00:00:39.360 --> 00:00:49.440
[Rebecca] There, in 194 and 195, dodging cows and passing interurban trolley cars, the Wright brothers developed the world's first fully practical flying machine.

7
00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.1000
[Rebecca] George brings us the remarkable, quiet genius of Huffman Prairie.

8
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:06.880
[Rebecca] When Orville and Wilbur Wright returned to their modest family home on Hawthorne Street in Dayton, Ohio, in late December 1903, they were not resting on their laurels.

9
00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:19.840
[George] The world remembered Kitty Hawk, four brief hops, the longest covering 852 feet in 59 seconds, ending when a sudden gust of wind flipped and wrecked the fragile Wright flyer on the sand.

10
00:01:19.840 --> 00:01:24.320
[George] The Wrights understood what the skeptical scientific establishment did not.

11
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:33.120
[George] Their 1903 machine was a delicate, underpowered laboratory prototype capable of flying only in straight lines into heavy ocean gales.

12
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.360
[George] It could not turn, it could not bank reliably, and it could not stay airborne for more than a minute.

13
00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:51.680
[George] To transform an experimental hop into a practical, marketable transportation technology that could circle above the earth, carry passengers, and land safely, they needed hundreds of flight tests.

14
00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:59.280
[George] But traveling 700 miles back to the isolated sand spits of the outer banks was expensive and logistically impractical.

15
00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:02.840
[George] They needed a flying field close to home in Ohio.

16
00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:13.800
[George] Back in their bicycle workshop, the brothers analyzed their kitty hawk telemetry, realizing that true flight control required dynamic equilibrium in three distinct dimensions.

17
00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:29.560
[George] Orville and Wilbur Wright returned to their bicycle workshop on West 3rd Street in Dayton, Ohio, with unshakable resolve, determined to transform their fragile coastal glider into a practical, controllable flying machine capable of everyday transport.

18
00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:42.920
[George] In the spring of 1904, Wilbur approached a prominent Dayton banker named Torrance Huffman, who owned an 84-acre pasture along the Dayton, Springfield and Urbana Electric Interurban Rail Line in Green County.

19
00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:53.640
[George] Huffman looked at the two quiet bicycle mechanics, warned them to avoid stampeding his herd of dairy cattle, and generously granted them permission to use the rough pasture rent-free.

20
00:02:53.640 --> 00:02:58.360
[George] Huffman Prairie was a far cry from the pristine, windy sands of Kitty Hawk.

21
00:02:58.360 --> 00:03:06.840
[George] The ground was hummocky, riddled with swampy wet holes, dotted with thorn trees, and covered in high, coarse prairie grass.

22
00:03:06.840 --> 00:03:14.920
[George] Worse still, unlike the steady, 20-mile-an-hour Atlantic Ocean breezes of Kitty Hawk that provided instant aerodynamic lift.

23
00:03:14.920 --> 00:03:24.840
[George] The summer winds over the Ohio pasture were sluggish, capricious, and unpredictable, frequently dropping to dead calms in the sweltering Midwestern heat.

24
00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:34.760
[George] Dairy cows frequently ambled across the launch path, forcing Wilbur to abort takeoff runs while Torrance Huffman looked on with amused indulgence from the fence rail.

25
00:03:34.760 --> 00:03:46.560
[George] Early settlers faced immense hardships carving farms and homesteads from the dense eastern woodlands, battling disease, inclement weather, and frontier isolation with unyielding resolve.

26
00:03:44.440 --> 00:03:59.280
[George] The field was crisscrossed by cow paths and dotted with thorn bushes, and the brothers had to shoe away grazing cattle and horses each morning before laying down their wooden launching rail into the prevailing western breeze.

27
00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:19.120
[George] In May 1904, having constructed a new, heavier biplane dubbed the Flyer 2 and erected a crude wooden storage shed at the edge of the pasture, the Wright brothers invited approximately forty distinguished guests and newspaper reporters from Cincinnati, Dayton, and Chicago to witness a public demonstration flight.

28
00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:27.360
[George] The brothers wanted to silence skeptics and confirm their invention, but the demonstration turned into a humiliating public fiasco.

29
00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:34.000
[George] In front of the gathering of skeptical journalists, the engine sputtered and misfired on the wooden launch rail.

30
00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:46.640
[George] On their second attempt, with winds dead calm, the aircraft sluggishly rumbled down the track, lifted barely six feet into the humid air, and flopped ignominiously into the grass after traveling fifty feet.

31
00:04:46.640 --> 00:04:53.440
[George] Disappointed journalists concluded that the Wrights were eccentric humbugs whose claims had been wildly exaggerated.

32
00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:57.600
[George] Reporters packed their notebooks and departed, never returning.

33
00:04:57.600 --> 00:05:12.400
[George] For the next 18 months, the Wrights conducted the greatest scientific revolution in transportation history in complete, unheralded obscurity, surrounded only by grazing cows and passing trolley riders who waved casually from the train windows.

34
00:05:12.400 --> 00:05:22.800
[George] The reporters who walked away in 1804 missed the greatest technological breakthrough in human history, falling victim to cynical skepticism when patience was required.

35
00:05:22.800 --> 00:05:33.480
[George] The spirit of American innovation was evident in every workshop and meeting hall, where practical mechanics and visionary thinkers sought solutions to the most challenging obstacles of their era.

36
00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:48.280
[George] Wilbur meticulously recorded every mechanical failure and control glitch in small pocket notebooks, analyzing the aerodynamic forces that caused the aircraft to sideslip and designing redesigned control linkages to stabilize the machine.

37
00:05:48.280 --> 00:06:01.800
[George] Recognizing that the calm Ohio air prevented their heavily loaded aircraft from achieving takeoff speed on a standard 100-foot wooden rail, the brothers devised an ingenious mechanical solution, the Derrick Catapult.

38
00:06:01.800 --> 00:06:08.760
[George] In September 1904, they erected a 16-foot wooden wooden derrick tower at the end of their rail track.

39
00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:15.000
[George] Hoisted to the top of the derrick was a 1600-pound counterweight of heavy stones and scrap iron.

40
00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:22.280
[George] A steel cable ran from the hanging weight, through pulleys beneath the rail, and hooked onto the front axle of the aircraft.

41
00:06:22.280 --> 00:06:36.760
[George] When the pilot released the trigger, the 1600-pound weight plummeted to the ground, yanking the aircraft down the launch rail like an arrow from a bowstring and hurling it into the air at 30 miles per hour, regardless of wind conditions.

42
00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:42.280
[George] With the catapult operating smoothly, flight experiments accelerated dramatically.

43
00:06:42.280 --> 00:06:48.600
[George] On September 20, 1904, Wilbur accomplished what had never before been achieved by man.

44
00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:58.440
[George] He banked the Flyer 2 into a graceful curve, executed a complete 360 degree circle in the sky, and landed safely back at the launch point.

45
00:06:58.440 --> 00:07:09.160
[George] The Derrick Catapult provided reliable 30-mile an hour launch velocity, freeing the brothers from their dependence on natural sea breezes and accelerating their flight trials.

46
00:07:09.160 --> 00:07:21.120
[George] News of these extraordinary events traveled by telegraph and steam train across the nation, captivating readers in major eastern cities and inspiring future generations of pioneers and inventors.

47
00:07:21.360 --> 00:07:34.480
[George] The addition of the 70-foot wooden derrick and falling counterweight transformed their flight testing program, providing reliable, instantaneous takeoff velocity regardless of whether the prairie air was calm or gusty.

48
00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.040
[George] Yet the aircraft remained dangerous and aerodynamically unstable.

49
00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:45.840
[George] In the autumn of 1904 and early 1905, the brothers suffered multiple terrifying crashes.

50
00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:56.320
[George] When attempting tight turns, the aircraft frequently lost airspeed, slipped sideways, and spun into the ground in violent tailspins that shattered the wooden airframe.

51
00:07:56.320 --> 00:08:04.640
[George] In the winter of 1904, the brothers dismantled the Flyer 2 and designed the masterpiece of their lives, the Wright Flyer 3.

52
00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.160
[George] Using their own wind tunnel research, they made radical aerodynamic modifications.

53
00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:17.200
[George] They separated the rudder controls from the wing warping system, giving the pilot independent three-axis control.

54
00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:27.200
[George] They increased the size of the forward elevator and moved it 12 feet forward of the wings, and enlarged the vertical twin rudders, moving them 12 feet to the rear.

55
00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:40.320
[George] Built with ash wood, spruce and muslin cloth, powered by an improved 25 horsepower four cylinder engine, the Flyer 3 was the world's first true, fully controllable, practical airplane.

56
00:08:40.320 --> 00:08:51.760
[George] In the wooden shed on the prairie, Orville and Wilbur spent winter nights testing wing ribs and propeller pitch, crafting mathematical tables that laid the foundation for modern aeronautics.

57
00:08:51.760 --> 00:09:02.920
[George] The enduring lessons of this historic chapter continue to resonate across American culture, reminding us of the courage, sacrifice and vision of those who shaped the destiny of our nation.

58
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:15.880
[George] Flyer 3 featured a completely independent rudder and wing warping system, giving the pilot full three-dimensional authority in the air and preventing the dangerous control reversal that had plagued earlier test flights.

59
00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:22.280
[George] In September and October 1905, Huffman Prairie became the stage for miraculous triumphs.

60
00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:31.640
[George] Wilbur and Orville flew daily, climbing to altitudes of 75 feet, weaving graceful figurates, and cruising smoothly above the treetops.

61
00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:40.200
[George] On October 3, 1905, Orville flew 21 miles in 32 minutes, landing only when his fuel was exhausted.

62
00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:46.280
[George] Two days later, on October 5, Wilbur took off, banked effortlessly around the pasture.

63
00:09:46.280 --> 00:09:59.800
[George] And stayed airborne for 39 minutes and 23 seconds, completing 30 laps of the field and covering over 24 miles, far longer than all 160 previous flights at Kitty Hawk and Dayton combined.

64
00:09:59.800 --> 00:10:04.600
[George] Local farmers paused their plowing to watch the mechanical bird wheel overhead.

65
00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:09.560
[George] Passengers on the electric trolley pressed their faces against the glass in disbelief.

66
00:10:09.560 --> 00:10:11.800
[George] The brothers had conquered the sky.

67
00:10:11.800 --> 00:10:19.480
[George] They could now remain airborne until fuel ran dry, fly wherever they chose, and land with the gentle touch of a bird.

68
00:10:19.480 --> 00:10:29.560
[George] Neighbors on the Eaton Pike stopped their buggies in awe, watching as a gasoline engine lifted a man above the trees and carried him across the sky in effortless circles.

69
00:10:29.560 --> 00:10:42.840
[George] Neighbors driving horse buggies along the Springfield Pike stopped in astonishment to watch Wilbur fly graceful circles overhead, marveling as the strange wooden craft banked and climbed above the trees without faltering.

70
00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:56.320
[George] Following the October 5th flight, the Wrights packed Flyer 3 into crates, ceased all flying, and spent three years negotiating patent rights and military contracts with the United States and European governments.

71
00:10:56.320 --> 00:11:02.800
[George] Returning to the world stage in 1908 to stun the planet with public flights in France and Virginia.

72
00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:11.360
[George] Today, Huffman Prairie is preserved as a National Historic Landmark, nestled within the secure perimeter of Wright Patterson Air Force Base.

73
00:11:11.360 --> 00:11:23.760
[George] Visitors can stand on the very ground where Wilbur banked into the first circle, walk past a replica of the wooden hangar and catapult Derrick, and watch modern supersonic Air Force jets thunder overhead.

74
00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:37.760
[George] It is a sacred landscape in American history, the humble Ohio cow pasture where two self-taught brothers from Dayton unlocked the secrets of the birds, mastered the air, and carried human civilization into the modern age of flight.

75
00:11:37.760 --> 00:11:48.400
[George] The humble Ohio Prairie is today celebrated as the true birthplace of practical aviation, where self-taught American mechanics proved that human beings could master the sky.

76
00:11:48.400 --> 00:12:02.080
[George] The unassuming pasture at Huffman Prairie is today enshrined as part of Dayton Aviation Heritage National Historical Park, revered by pilots worldwide as the sacred crucible where humanity mastered the art of practical flight.

77
00:12:02.080 --> 00:12:16.160
[George] The enduring legacy of their Dayton experiments proved that perseverance, rigorous scientific methodology, and unwavering self-reliance could conquer the oldest dream of humankind, opening the boundless highways of the sky.

78
00:12:16.720 --> 00:12:24.160
[Rebecca] The Wright brothers' relentless discipline at Huffman Prairie transformed an impossible dream into practical reality.

79
00:12:24.160 --> 00:12:25.520
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

80
00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:32.360
[Rebecca] Join us next week as we journey into the rugged hills of Kentucky for the legendary hath McCoy Field.

